1. IIS — enable IIS metrics
On the WEB01 VM, first check the exporter service command/config:
Get-CimInstance Win32_Service -Filter "Name='windows_exporter'" |
Select-Object Name,PathName

Then open:
C:\Program Files\windows_exporter\config.yaml

Make the collectors line include iis:
collectors:
  enabled: "[defaults],iis"

Restart:
Restart-Service windows_exporter

Verify:
Invoke-WebRequest http://localhost:9182/metrics -UseBasicParsing |
Select-String "windows_iis"

The IIS collector exposes things including total requests, 404s, current worker requests, and error counts by HTTP status code. GitHub
Then your Perses queries can be:
Request rate
sum(rate(windows_iis_requests_total{
  namespace="vm-lab",
  service="web01-metrics"
}[5m]))

404 rate
sum(rate(windows_iis_not_found_errors_total{
  namespace="vm-lab",
  service="web01-metrics"
}[5m]))

403 rate
sum(rate(windows_iis_worker_request_errors_total{
  namespace="vm-lab",
  service="web01-metrics",
  status_code="403"
}[5m]))

5xx rate
sum(rate(windows_iis_worker_request_errors_total{
  namespace="vm-lab",
  service="web01-metrics",
  status_code=~"5.."
}[5m]))

2. SQL Server — blocked/hung activity
On DB01, enable the MSSQL collector:
collectors:
  enabled: "[defaults],mssql"

Restart:
Restart-Service windows_exporter

Verify:
Invoke-WebRequest http://localhost:9182/metrics -UseBasicParsing |
Select-String "windows_mssql"

Windows Exporter’s MSSQL collector includes blocked processes, active transactions, lock waits, deadlocks and database log usage. GitHub
Useful panels:
Blocked processes
windows_mssql_genstats_blocked_processes{
  namespace="vm-lab",
  service="db01-metrics"
}

Active transactions — LegacyOrders
windows_mssql_databases_active_transactions{
  namespace="vm-lab",
  service="db01-metrics",
  database="LegacyOrders"
}

Deadlocks
sum(rate(windows_mssql_locks_deadlocks{
  namespace="vm-lab",
  service="db01-metrics"
}[5m]))

Lock waits
sum(rate(windows_mssql_locks_lock_waits{
  namespace="vm-lab",
  service="db01-metrics"
}[5m]))

For a true “transaction running > 60 seconds” panel, Windows performance counters are not enough. Later we can add a small SQL exporter query against SQL Server DMVs to expose long-running/blocked sessions directly.
